{"skill_id":"led","version":3,"questions":[{"id":"led-r1-q4","version":1,"round":1,"type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps that say what makes a band gap direct. Each step after the first uses the result of the step before it.","explanation":"A photon cannot make up a large change in crystal momentum. When the two band edges already share a momentum, the electron can drop and emit the photon alone. An indirect gap needs a phonon to supply the difference, so radiative recombination is rare.","steps":["A photon carries almost no momentum.","A transition that emits a photon must keep the electron's momentum nearly the same.","That is possible when the conduction-band minimum and the valence-band maximum occur at the same momentum.","That alignment is a direct gap, and recombination can then emit a photon by itself."]},{"id":"led-r1-q2","round":1,"type":"numeric","prompt":"Using \\(\\lambda = 1.24/E_g\\) (λ in µm, \\(E_g\\) in eV), what wavelength (in nm) does a material with \\(E_g = 1.9\\ \\mathrm{eV}\\) emit?","answer":652.632,"unit":"nm","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"\\(\\lambda = 1.24/1.9 = 0.653\\ \\mu\\mathrm{m} = 653\\ \\mathrm{nm}\\), red light.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 350 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Direct band gap E-k diagram: the conduction-band minimum sits directly above the valence-band maximum, so a vertical (photon-only) transition connects them.\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" style=\"max-width:350px;width:100%;height:auto\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"170\"/><polygon points=\"330,170 323.6,172.7 323.6,167.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"20\"/><polygon points=\"40,20 42.7,26.4 37.3,26.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">k</te