entColor\" stroke=\"none\">photon</text></svg>","figure_alt":"Direct band gap E-k diagram: the conduction-band minimum sits directly above the valence-band maximum, so a vertical (photon-only) transition connects them."},{"id":"led-r1-q5","version":1,"round":1,"type":"error","prompt":"A worked solution says whether silicon makes a good LED. Exactly one step is wrong.","explanation":"The indirect gap is why a photon alone cannot finish the transition. Radiative recombination is rare, so silicon turns the recombination energy into heat rather than light.","steps":["Silicon's conduction-band minimum and valence-band maximum occur at different momenta.","A photon cannot supply that momentum difference.","Radiative recombination is therefore rare, and most recombination makes heat.","Silicon is therefore an efficient LED, because every recombination emits a photon."],"flaw_index":3,"reasons":["The indirect gap makes radiative recombination rare, so silicon is a poor LED material.","Every recombination emits a photon, so the momentum difference does not matter."],"correct_reason":0},{"id":"led-r2-q1","round":2,"type":"numeric","prompt":"What band gap (in eV) is needed for a blue LED at \\(\\lambda = 460\\ \\mathrm{nm}\\)?","answer":2.69565,"unit":"eV","tolerance":0.02,"explanation":"\\(E_g = 1.24/0.46 \\approx 2.70\\ \\mathrm{eV}\\). Wider gaps give shorter wavelengths.","figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 350 190\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Direct band gap E-k diagram: the conduction-band minimum sits directly above the valence-band maximum, so a vertical (photon-only) transition connects them.\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" style=\"max-width:350px;width:100%;height:auto\"><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"170\"/><polygon points=\"330,170 323.6,172.7 323.6,167.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x